course: dictionary
lesson: 5589
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives each meaning a number, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word liberate is a good example. It can mean to set something free, to free someone from a limit, to release gas or energy in science, or, as a joke, to steal.
explain: In history, an army can liberate a town. This means the army drives out the enemy who had taken control, so the people are free again. «In autumn 2022 Ukraine liberated much of the Kharkiv region» uses liberate in this serious sense.
explain: Liberate can also mean to free someone from something that holds them back, such as rules or worries. A liberating feeling is the happy feeling of being free, like the moment after your last test of the year.
explain: In science, a chemical reaction can liberate heat or gas, which means it releases them. Joking slang is different: «"Liberate" is a jokey way to say steal.» So if someone asks, «Who liberated my biscuits?», they mean who took them. Check the context before you decide which meaning is right.
fact: Liberate means to set free.
fact: «Liberate» is a jokey way to say steal.
fact: In science, «the reaction liberates heat» means the reaction releases heat.
fact: Useful phrases are liberate from and a liberating feeling.
fact: Many everyday words have more than one meaning, so always check the words around them.
exercise: «The army came to liberate the town from the enemy.» What does liberate mean here? | *To set free from the enemy | To paint the houses bright colours | To go to sleep in the town | To build a new wall around the town
exercise: Ben reads that the reaction «liberates heat». What is happening? | *Heat is being released | Heat is being stolen by a thief | Heat is being measured with a ruler | Heat is being hidden under a blanket
exercise: Sam’s sister says, «Who liberated my biscuits?» What does she mean? | Who set them free from the tin | Who bought them at the shop | *Who took them without asking | Who baked them for the party
test: When an army drives out an enemy from a town, which word fits best? | *liberate | decorate | celebrate | hibernate
test: Finishing the last test gave Sam a ___ feeling of freedom. Which word fits the gap? | liberate | liberates | *liberating
exam: Which sentence uses liberate as a joke for steal? | The army liberated the town from the enemy | The reaction liberates heat into the room | *Who liberated my biscuits from the tin?
